                "text": "I'm trying to decode the grid transformation, looking at where shapes sit in each row and how the markers on the left (3,2,2,3) relate to shifting the shape values in the output rows.\n\nI notice the leftmost cell in each shifted shape row becomes 1, and the 5 appears at a position offset from that start\u2014so the 1 and 5 seem tied to each row's local shift point rather than fixed columns, and I'm testing whether the offset from the shifted start is consistent (1 at offset0, 5 at offset3) across rows 4-7.\n\nWait, row3 actually shows no markers at all, just uniform color, so rows without a shift marker stay unshifted and unmarked. Checking rows5,6 with a right-shift of 1, the markers land at col4 and col7 within the shape's new span \u2014 consistent with 1 at col4, 5 at col7. So the pattern seems to be: the left-column value dictates a per-row shift (3=shift left, 2=shift right), and the marker pixels move along with the shape.\n\nStill not sure if the shift amount is fixed or depends on the marker's color value, so I think I should test this by constructing a small query example with a simple rectangle and varied marker types to check.",
